 "article": {
  "title": "A Second Pair of Eyes: How AI Helps Doctors",
  "topic": "科技（医疗人工智能）",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 325,
  "paras": [
   "Every day, hospitals produce mountains of images: X-rays, CT scans, eye photographs, skin pictures. A single doctor may look at hundreds of them in one shift, and tired eyes do miss things. In recent years, hospitals in many countries have begun to use software that can read some of these images on its own. The technology does not replace the doctor. Instead, it acts like a second pair of eyes that never gets sleepy.",
   "The idea is simple. A computer program is shown millions of past images, each with a note saying what was wrong. Slowly it learns the patterns that human experts look for: a shadow here, an unusual shape there. When a new picture arrives, the program marks the places that worry it and gives a score. In one eye clinic, the system reads photographs of the retina and warns doctors about early signs of disease that are easy to miss.",
   "Yet the news is not all good. The programs are only as good as the data they were trained on. If the training images come mostly from one country or one age group, the program may work badly for everyone else. It can also be confidently wrong, and a confident machine may mislead a busy doctor. For this reason, hospitals keep a specialist in the loop: the software suggests, the human decides. Patients, too, want to know when a machine has played a part; some hospitals now print a short line on the report saying that a computer was used.",
   "So where does this leave us? Most experts agree that the future is not man against machine, but man with machine. The computer is fast, tireless and cheap; the doctor is careful, creative and kind. Used together, they can catch disease earlier and free doctors to spend more time with the people in front of them. That is a trade worth making, as long as we never forget who is in charge."
  ],
  "zh": "医院每天产生海量影像，医生一个人一轮班要看几百张，疲劳的眼睛难免漏掉问题。近年不少医院开始用能自己读片的软件——它不取代医生，而像一双永不疲倦的眼睛。原理是用上百万张带标注的旧影像训练程序，让它学会专家关注的那些模式；新片子进来，它标出可疑处并给出评分。但软件的质量取决于训练数据：如果数据大多来自一个国家或一个年龄段，它对其他人可能表现很差，而且它会“自信地出错”，误导忙碌的医生。所以医院让专科医生始终在回路里——软件建议，人做决定；患者也有权知道机器参与了多少。结论不是“人机对抗”，而是“人机协作”：机器快、不知疲倦、成本低，医生细致、有创造力、有温度，结合起来才能更早发现疾病，也让医生有时间多陪病人。",
  "hard": [
   {
    "en": "Instead, it acts like a second pair of eyes that never gets sleepy.",
    "cn": "相反，它像一双永不疲倦的眼睛。",
    "why": "that 引导定语从句修饰 eyes；instead 表示与前句相反，四级阅读常见的转折衔接。"
   },
   {
    "en": "If the training images come mostly from one country or one age group, the program may work badly for everyone else.",
    "cn": "如果训练图像大多来自某一个国家或某一个年龄段，这个程序对其他人可能表现很差。",
    "why": "if 引导条件状语从句；everyone else 暗含比较，点出“以偏概全”的风险。"
   },
   {
    "en": "Used together, they can catch disease earlier and free doctors to spend more time with the people in front of them.",
    "cn": "两者结合起来用，能更早发现疾病，也让医生有时间多陪眼前的人。",
    "why": "过去分词短语 Used together 作状语；free sb to do sth（使某人有空做某事）是很好用的写作搭配。"
   }
  ],
